Description

This week, we used Qwen-Image-2.1 as the executor and reproduced the frozen region-editing protocol locally. We evaluated reference matching on 21 image pairs, using AITone baseline outputs as inputs and iPhone images as references. The experiment compared region-constrained and global prompts, with SoftISP redraw as an additional baseline.
1. Reproduced the eight-condition region-editing protocol with seed 42 and archived the experiment records, manifests, and reports.
2. Compared region-constrained and global prompts on 21 pairs using quantitative metrics, region-mask checks, and visual comparison grids.
Results

Figure 1. Visual comparison
Each row shows the AITone input, iPhone reference, SoftISP redraw, Qwen output with a region-constrained prompt, and Qwen output with a global prompt. Across the 21 pairs, both Qwen settings largely preserve the input framing and content while making relatively conservative adjustments. SoftISP follows the reference exposure more closely in some cases, but occasionally produces undesirable results, such as over-darkening in pair 3.

Figure 2. Region-constrained vs. global prompts
The plots compare per-pair face and background changes (left), alignment of the face-to-background ratio with the reference (center), and warmth alignment with the reference (right). The distributions overlap substantially, indicating that the tested region-constraint wording had little measurable effect under these experimental conditions. More explicit spatial guidance, such as masks or region annotations, is a candidate for improving region-level control and should be evaluated separately.

